# How to cite Dedalo.
#
# GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from this file, and Zenodo
# reads it if the repository is ever archived there. It exists because this
# project makes checkable claims — exhaustive proofs over the fee schedule, a
# hash-chained ledger, a stated threat model — and that is the kind of thing
# that gets cited in work on funding mechanisms or supply-chain provenance.
# A citation derived from this file is right; one derived from a URL in a
# footnote rots.
#
# `version` and `date-released` are updated by scripts/bump-version.sh, which
# is the only thing allowed to change a version in this repository.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Dedalo in academic work, please cite it as below."
title: "Dedalo: deterministic merge-to-earn funding for open source"
abstract: >-
  Dedalo turns git merges into contributor payouts. Attribution, the fee split
  and the payout plan are deterministic and offline: the same history and
  configuration produce a byte-identical, content-addressed plan on any
  machine. Money is held as integers of base units and percentages as basis
  points; splits use the largest-remainder method and conserve every base
  unit. Settlement is a pull model — a round is deposited once against a
  Merkle root and contributors claim — and the tool holds no signing key,
  proposing transactions for people to sign rather than broadcasting them.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Dedalo contributors"
    website: "https://github.com/dedalo-org/dedalo/graphs/contributors"
repository-code: "https://github.com/dedalo-org/dedalo"
url: "https://dedalo-org.github.io/dedalo/"
license: MIT
version: 0.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-26"
keywords:
  - open-source-funding
  - merge-to-earn
  - git
  - reproducible-builds
  - attribution
  - payouts
